{{also|Modus Operandi}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*med-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|la|modus operandī}}, from {{m|la|modus||manner, method}} and {{m|la|operandī||of working}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈməʊ.dʌs ˌɒp.ə.ɹæn.daɪ/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmɐʉ.dɘs ˌɔp.ɘ.ɹɛn.dɒe/|/ˈmɐʉ.dɘs ˌɔp.ɘ.ɹɛn.di/ |a=NZ}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈmoʊ.dɘs ˌɑp.əɹ.ɛn.daɪ/|/ˈmoʊ.dɘs ˌɑp.əɹ.ɛn.di/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-NaomiAmethyst-modus operandi.wav|a=US}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|modi operandi|s|nolinkhead=1}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A person or thing's normal mode of operation.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2001|author=w:Eoin Colfer|title=Artemis Fowl|pages=92–93|passage=Failing that, if contact was absolutely necessary, a series of thumb jabs to the nerve cluster at the base of the neck would be his chosen '''modus operandi'''—quiet as a whisper.}}
# {{lb|en|law enforcement}} A known criminal's established habits and mode of work when committing specific offences, especially fraud, matched with characteristics of an unsolved crime to narrow down (limit to a specific list) or [[profile]] suspects.

